    "narrative": "This program executes a multi-stage rootkit installation sequence with three distinct phases: **reconnaissance**, **privilege escalation**, and **persistence + shell spawning**.\n\nIn Phase 1, the program opens `/proc/modules` and reads the kernel module list — a classic defensive environment check. Rootkits use this to detect security monitoring tools (SELinux, AppArmor, auditd) before proceeding.\n\nIn Phase 2, the program opens `/dev/mem` (raw physical memory) and `/dev/kmem` (kernel virtual memory) with read-write access. It issues an undocumented `ioctl` request (code 0x4001) to the memory device and calls `setuid(0)` to become root. It then seeks `/dev/kmem` to the kernel module memory region (0xffffffffa8000000) and **writes 64 bytes of payload directly into kernel memory** — installing a kernel-level hook.\n\nIn Phase 3, with root privileges established and the kernel patched, the program spawns `/bin/sh` via `execve`, giving the attacker a **fully privileged interactive root shell** with the kernel hook persistent across the session.",
